myself heads who of you likes free food  perfect because free pizza changed my  life it was a cold October evening my  freshman year at college and I was in  the main building of my university the  Warsaw School of Economics finishing up  the last day of a five day marathon  called Student Union elections campaign  now I had dedicated all my time to this  ever since high school I wanted to  become a policymaker and work in  Brussels in the European Union  structures so I was dedicated and  becoming a member of my University  Student Union seemed like a natural next  step to achieving my professional goal  nothing else mattered so let October  evening is I was packing up my things I  bumped into my friend coincidentally  named me Hall as well he said I'm hungry  let's grab a slice of pizza that they  have in the room upstairs in one of the  student clubs meetings he said it would  take only a minute now I was exhausted I  was exhausted to the point that I think  actually snoozed when he was telling me  this but somehow I agreed  unfortunately that minute turned into a  dreadful hour a dreadful presentation  that I had to sit through the  presentation was called consulting the  best job in the world I was really angry  at him and as soon as they finished I  just stormed out of the room I went back  home as I got back home I opened my  laptop and there was an email from him  saying well you left before they started  signing people up for projects so I  signed you up for one now I was not  happy or thankful but I am happy today I  am happy that I bumped into him I am  happy that I had this hour-long slice of  pizza and I am thankful that he signed  me up for this project because of this  project I actually joined the consulting  Club and the next year I became its  president in this club I first heard the  name Procter &amp; Gamble and I fell in love  with marketing which led to my career in  brand management and eventually got me  here  so the slice of pizza that I had no  rational reason to go for at the moment  actually inflicted series of events that  changed my life choices now ever since  then I've had many more serendipitous  events in my life and each single one of  them led to the happiest memories and  the closest relationships that I cherish  and hole deep in my heart now to me this  is the true impact of serendipity why am  I telling you this  because I know that each single one of  you has had at least one serendipitous  event in your life even though you might  not know what you did to make this  opportunity happen so today I want to  share with you four suggestions that  helped me increase their independence  and hopefully will help you as well so  number one just turn up when I was  working in Procter &amp; Gamble in Geneva  there was a small digital media agency  they reach out to me and ask me if I can  have an hour meeting with them I didn't  want to for three reasons I wasn't  looking for a digital agency I was  swamped with work and it was the last  week before the three-week vacation that  I had booked a year later a year earlier  but again somehow I agreed we had to  call exchanged emails did not talk for a  year after a year I got shipped back to  Poland and I was in need of a digital  agency so I called them up I hired them  we did the project it was super  successful so I went out for beers with  one of the co-founders after two or  three beers I think I became a little  bit too sincere with him because because  I told him that I'm actually really not  happy with my current position at P&amp;G  and I want to change something in my  professional life I wasn't looking for  advice but what he said next just hit me  like a rock he said why don't you get an  MBA you know what he was one of only  five GSB grads that lived in Poland at  that time were the chances right now he  helped me with the application wrote one  of the recommendation letters and the  rest is history so we have to often find  ourselves being invited to meetings  at least my default is to decline when I  don't see an immediate benefit but I  challenge you just as I challenge myself  to make just turn up a rule number to  put yourself in the right place so this  happened when I was junior at my college  and I wanted to spend my summer in  marketing at a CPG company the best in  the world not Pepsi PNG I'm sorry sorry  Procter &amp; Gamble now the tough part is  that they had a limited number of slots  and it was really hard to get hold of  marketing managers so what I did is I  applied to an IT case study of course  with no prior IT background somehow I  got in now when I walked into the room  and everyone started introducing  themselves  I really felt out of place because there  are 29 CS and engineering majors and me  marketing and management major but you  know what one of the panelists was a  brand manager and I was the only person  interested in marketing so I just  approached him after the case study I  told him hey can we grab a coffee  this coffee led to me getting an  interview and eventually getting an  internship so per freezing famous Steve  Ballmer's presentation to developers  location location location  if you want to find relevant people to  your cause make sure you put yourself  right in the middle number three you  certainly pretty engines I started my  blog in July 2013 just before coming to  the GSB and throughout the last one and  a half years I've had more people reach  out to me than throughout the previous  28 years of my life in fact one person  wrote to me that my blog was one of the  reasons that he applied to the GSB he  got in and he's coming next fall so who  knows maybe he'll become my future  co-founder and fellow billionaire but my  point is here that there's so many of  those engines and you can name them  WordPress medium Twitter Facebook and I  think it's a sin not to be using them  because they're out there 24/7 so they  create this amazing background hum for  for opportunity and and for serendipity  and last but not least is don't require  a reason particularly in business school  I find myself needing a reason for every  single thing I do in fact last year I  signed up for a disco class called  leadership through flow people asked me  is like why did you do that and I told  them like I don't know and to be honest  with you I still have no clue why I did  that but it was refreshing it was fun  and I learned that such seemingly  insignificant thing as posture can have  a major impact on how people perceive  your leadership skills so it's okay to  say I don't know why it's okay to do  things even if you don't know why  because the experience and the knowledge  that you can get out of them can benefit  you in the most unexpected of  circumstances now there's an obvious  danger there's an obvious danger with  serendipity there's an obvious danger in  chasing those open-ended opportunities  that might not have have an immediate  benefit in your life the danger is that  you get distracted from your day-to-day  work and you become frustrated but you  know what if you don't open your life  for serendipity you'll get stuck in a  rot  and you'll become miserable so what I  think the solution is is to find the  Goldilocks zone of not too little not  too much just enough so that the next  time your friend tells you there is a  free pizza at an event take him or her  on it because that free pizza can change  your life it surely changed mine thank  you
 

